{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You shall not covet.</b> It is known that coveting depends upon the heart, and the essence of the commandment is that a person should despair of all that his fellow has — whether real estate or movable property — and turn his heart away from this thought, so that he neither thinks about them nor covets them. For through coveting a person may come to murder, as we find in the case of Achan (Joshua 7:21): ״ואראה בשלל אדרת שנער אחת טובה״ — 'and I saw among the spoil a fine Shinar mantle' — ״ואחמדם ואקחם״ — 'and I coveted them and took them,' and this coveting brought him to stoning. And so we find in the matter of Ahab, who coveted the vineyard of Naboth the Jezreelite, and it was the cause that brought him to death." "<b>And they said</b> in the Midrash: Whoever covets what is not fitting for him — what he covets is not given to him, and what is in his hand is taken from him. For so we find in the case of the primordial serpent: he set his eyes upon Eve and coveted her, and she was not fitting for him; what he sought was not given to him, and what was in his hand was taken from him — for he was cursed on this account: ״על גחונך תלך״ — 'upon your belly you shall go.' Thus far in the Midrash. And were it not that robbery is included within coveting, and from the side of coveting it would have been fitting to write among the Ten Utterances among the prohibitions, the prohibition of 'you shall not rob,' for it is a very grave transgression and most of the world stumble in it, and furthermore it is a rationally understood commandment like murder and adultery and theft and false testimony — but because coveting includes it, since a person does not rob until he covets, as it is written (Micah 2:2): ״וחמדו שדות וגזלו״ — 'and they coveted fields and robbed' — therefore there was no need to mention it separately, and it was included within the prohibition of 'you shall not covet.' And furthermore, because He wished to forbid even coveting, which is the desire of the heart, at all times and in every hour, even though it does not come to the point of deed, so that a person would learn an a fortiori argument (קל וחומר) to forbid robbery, which is the deed. And indeed we find a coveting that is permitted, and that is the coveting of Torah and commandments, as the Sages of blessed memory said: ״קנאת סופרים תרבה חכמה״ — 'jealousy among scholars increases wisdom' — and this coveting and jealousy is permitted, and a person receives reward for it. And included within permitted coveting is one who covets his fellow's daughter for his son by way of marriage. For we learned (Exodus 20:14): ״לא תחמוד בית רעך״ — a general principle; (Deuteronomy 5:18): ״שדהו ועבדו ואמתו שורו וחמורו״ — the specifics. Just as the specifics are things that can only come into your possession with the consent and permission of their owners, so too the general principle — your daughter for your son, or his son for your daughter, which can come into your possession without the permission of their father. Behold, I have explained for you the five latter Utterances that are on the second tablet — their arrangement and their explanation, and their connection one to another is as follows: He began with 'You shall not murder,' and warned not to diminish human beings, even though they are many. And lest a person say, since he is warned not to diminish them, he may multiply them in prohibition and in the way of harlotry — therefore He immediately warned, 'You shall not commit adultery.' And lest he say, since He warned not to diminish them and not to multiply them in prohibition — well then, he may kidnap human beings, which neither diminishes them nor reduces them, but merely leads them to another place to sell, and this is not against their persons but against their property — therefore He said, 'You shall not steal.' And lest he say, kidnapping of persons, even though it does not diminish them, is forbidden because it harms through deed, but to harm through speech is permitted — therefore He said, 'You shall not bear false witness.' And since He forbade speech, perhaps he will think that the evil thought is permitted, to covet in his heart his fellow's property and all that is his — therefore it says, 'You shall not covet,' and it comes to forbid even the thought." "<b>And the reason</b> that the Utterances were ten — by way of the plain sense (peshat): because it is a complete number that embraces everything, and it is the culmination of all numbering, for there is no number in the world that is not included within the letter י (ten). <br><b>By way of the Midrash:</b> the Utterances were ten because previously the generations had been commanded in seven commandments, and afterwards they rose to nine, and now the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to bestow merit upon Israel and multiplied commandments corresponding to all of them, and gave them everything. For so they said in the Midrash: a parable — to what may the matter be compared? To a king who had a great cellar, and within it many barrels of fine wine. His friend came — he gave him one barrel. A second came — he gave him two. After a time a third came — he gave him three. A fourth came — he gave him four. After a time the king's son came — he gave him the entire cellar. So too, when the first man was created, he was commanded concerning six commandments, so that the world would be sustained by its six directions. Noah came and was commanded concerning the seventh — this is a limb from a living animal. Abraham — concerning the eighth, this is circumcision. Jacob — concerning the ninth, this is the sinew of the thigh. When Israel came, He gave them the Ten Utterances, which embrace 613 commandments. And concerning this the verse said regarding the offerings of the twelve princes (Numbers 7): ״כף אחת עשרה זהב מלאה קטרת״ — one dish of ten gold, full of incense — full of תרי״ג, for the letter ק is interchangeable with ד, and this Midrash explains to us that the תרי״ג letters in the Ten Utterances are the 613 commandments included within them." "<b>By way of reason (sekhel):</b> the Utterances were ten corresponding to the ten spheres (גלגלים). The first utterance — 'I am' — corresponds to the sphere of the intellect (and you should be roused to this). 'You shall have no other Elohim' corresponds to the all-encompassing sphere (גלגל המקיף), and it mentions in it 'other Elohim,' because many thought of it that it is the Creator, because it is not a body. 'You shall not take' corresponds to the sphere of the sun. 'Remember' corresponds to the sphere of the moon. 'Honor' corresponds to the sphere of Jupiter (צדק). 'You shall not murder' corresponds to the sphere of Mars (מאדים). 'You shall not commit adultery' corresponds to the sphere of Saturn (שבתאי). 'You shall not steal' corresponds to the sphere of Venus (נוגה). 'You shall not bear false witness' corresponds to the sphere of the constellations (גלגל המזלות). 'You shall not covet' corresponds to the sphere of Mercury (גלגל כוכב). And there is great arousal in aligning each utterance against each sphere, and if you examine them you will find the matter pointing to the wonders of the Name 'who was and is and will be' (הש״י) and the wisdom of His formations. And there is another matter: they were ten corresponding to the ten root organs that exist in a person, which are the essence of his vitality and the root of his structure. For we find the structure of man like the structure of Torah: in Torah there are 248 positive commandments, 365 negative commandments, and 10 Utterances; and so in the structure of man there are 248 organs, 365 sinews, and 10 root organs. And if you examine them you will find them aligned against the Ten Utterances — each utterance aligned against each organ appointed over it and entrusted to it. And these are: the heart, the ear, the mouth, the brain, the hands, the liver, the covenant of circumcision, the feet, the eyes, and the kidneys. And if you are aroused to examine them in their alignment, you will find them." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> the Ten Utterances were against the ten sefirot, and you will find in them תרי״ג letters corresponding to the תרי״ג commandments that are emanated and drawn forth from them. And the seven additional letters beyond the תרי״ג that are in them indicate that the תרי״ג letters and the תרי״ג commandments were given from within the seven known ones that I mentioned above, and the seven letters together with the תרי״ג complete כת״ר letters, for so are the seven emanated from the Keter." "<b>And he answered.</b> I shall make you understand and guide you in this path: I shall set forth here each commandment corresponding to which sefirah. The first commandment, 'I am,' corresponds to Keter; 'You shall have no other god' corresponds to Ḥokhmah; 'You shall not take' corresponds to Tiferet; 'Remember' corresponds to Atarah, which is Knesset Yisrael; 'Honor' corresponds to Ḥesed; 'You shall not murder' corresponds to Pahad; 'You shall not commit adultery' corresponds to Yesod; 'You shall not steal' corresponds to Nezah; 'You shall not answer' corresponds to Teshuvah; 'You shall not covet' corresponds to Hod. And you must acquire understanding and contemplate the intention, even though the sefirot are not in the same order as the commandments." "<b>And you must</b> know that the reason the commandment 'I am' was fixed first is because it is the commandment of unity — to believe in the First Being, who is first to all firsts. And 'You shall have no other god' was fixed in the second commandment, because even though the angels exist first in creation, they are nonetheless in the rank of secondhood relative to the existence of the Blessed Name; therefore they were created on the second day, and from this they are called שניינים (secondary ones). And the commandment 'You shall not take' was fixed third, because one who bears His name in vain destroys the world, which stands on three things — on Torah, on worship, and on acts of kindness — and he is punished with three destructions, for he consumes himself, his trees, and his stones. And He repeated and mentioned 'אשר ישא את שמו לשוא' — 'who bears His name in vain' — in order to hint at the magnitude of the transgression, because one who stumbles in it takes lightly the honor of the Blessed Name and casts off from himself the yoke of honor and fear; and this is what the prophet said (Malachi 1:6): ״ואם אב אני איה כבודי ואם אדונים אני איה מוראי״. 'Remember' is in the fourth, because the Sabbath day is fourth to the fourth day, and this corresponds to the luminaries that were created on the fourth day: for the sun is in the fourth sphere, and likewise the moon is in the fourth sphere to the sun, according to צ״ם חנכ״ל, for the luminaries are fourfold; and likewise Saturn (Shabbetai), which is the star of the Sabbath, you will find to be fourth to the sphere of the Intellect, for above Saturn is the eighth sphere in which the stars are, and above it the ninth sphere, which is Aravot, and above Aravot the sphere of the Intellect; and so the matters of the Sabbath are fourfold — four prayers on the Sabbath, none resembling the other; and likewise the words of the prophet concerning the Sabbath are fourfold, as he said (Isaiah 58:13): ״אם תשיב משבת רגליך עשות חפציך ביום קדשי״. 'Honor' is in the fifth, because the nature of human reproduction is drawn from five forces, and they are: the father, the mother, the elements, time, and the Holy One, blessed be He. The father — from him come the sinews and bones, all that is white is in him. The mother — from her come all the red parts of the human, such as flesh, blood, hair, and the black of the eye; and this black is actually red but was afflicted. The elements — they are the four elements from which every composite is sustained by their combination. Time — that the time pass and the span of days that are fitting be prolonged upon him, for then the work is complete. The Holy One, blessed be He — He breathes into him a soul, which is the principal thing for making him wise and giving him understanding, for without it the prior operations have no substance. And likewise we find in the nature of the offspring of living creatures that they are five kinds: humans, beasts, birds, fish, and reptiles. And in this fifth commandment, the earth is mentioned in regard to the nature of their offspring — five things: a field of grain, a field of vineyards, a vegetable garden, an orchard, a forest growing trees. Also the kinds of metals found in the earth are five: gold, silver, copper, iron, and lead. 'You shall not murder' is in the sixth, because there are six traits that cause murder, and they are: jealousy, lust, hatred, anger, frivolity, and drunkenness; and the murderer kills the human who was created on the sixth day. 'You shall not commit adultery' is in the seventh, because the forbidden sexual relations are seven, and they are: a married woman, a menstruant wife, a widow, a virgin, lying with an animal, lying with a male, and a Cuthean woman. And seven matters are forbidden in it, and they are: looking, listening, seclusion, speech, touch, kissing, and the act. 'You shall not steal' is in the eighth, because the categories of theft are eight, and they are: in calculation, in measure, in weight, in a found object, one who kidnaps a person from his brother, one who steals money, one who steals the mind (deception), and one who steals for himself — that is, one who becomes a guardian of orphans and holds onto their money with permission, for he arranges to marry his son to the orphan girl; and concerning him it is said (Leviticus 19:13): ״לא תעשק את רעך ולא תגזל״. 'You shall not answer' is in the ninth — it is known that the ninth sphere is Aravot, in which is the Throne of Glory, and there is the treasury of souls; and one who testifies falsely denies that place, which is the special property of souls, so that his soul will not return on high. 'You shall not covet' is in the tenth, because covetousness is the affliction of the workers of iniquity; and since he covets others' money, all the more so will he covet his own money not to separate from it the tithe; and therefore the commandment 'You shall not covet' was fixed in the tenth, to warn concerning the tithe, for one who covets steals, and whoever steals the tithes for himself steals them, for the earth will flatter him (תחניף לו); and because of this it was necessary to fix the tenth commandment as 'You shall not covet.' And with this commandment the Ten Commandments were completed, corresponding to the ten spheres and the ten sefirot aforementioned. And the fact that they were not written in the book in the order of the spheres and the wondrous sefirot — this is a testimony and a sign to the statement of the sages who know awesome secrets: there is no earlier and later in the Torah of YHWH of Hosts. Through them our Elohim has magnified us and bequeathed us preeminence and merit; through them He set upon our heads the crown of kingship; there all the exalted wisdoms — mysteries and profound secrets; there תרי״ג (613) commandments are inscribed; there all the letters are sealed — except for the letter tet, which is hidden from there and is not mentioned. And the reason for this, according to the sages in their received tradition, who know the Ten Commandments on their foundations, is that the tablets were destined to be shattered, and so that they would not say that its goodness had ceased from them; and their intention in this is because the letter tet alludes to good (טוב), and the first tet in the Torah is inscribed in the word טוב." "<b>And according to</b> my opinion, the reason the letter tet is missing — for it alludes to the tenth stone, the crown's crown — is because of the prohibition of leaven (חמץ) on Passover, for by His great name He brought us out of Egypt from darkness to light; and likewise at Mount Sinai, by His great name the Torah was given. And a support for my words is what they hinted at in the Midrash of the darshanim of inscriptions (דורשי רשומות), for the word טיט״א is a sign of killing (הריגה) in the ancient tongue, and the discerning one will understand, investigate, and listen, and from this he will believe — for the letter tet alludes to the attribute of judgment (מדת הדין). And do not be astonished if it is mentioned in the second tablets, for so it is fitting, because that commandment — 'You shall not covet' — is guarded; we were warned in it to keep a strict watch, and all is founded on the pedestals of reason and understanding, and the commandments are hewn from the source of supernal Wisdom and Understanding. Happy are the ears that heard them. And its end is like its beginning, for it began with a commandment of the heart and concluded with a commandment of HaShem, in the manner of the statement of Eitan ha-Ezrahi in his book, wherein the righteous are crowned, and the discerning ones shine like the brightness of the firmament — concerning the matter of the ten sefirot beli-mah, which are the beginning of every undertaking and the head of all dominion, their end being embedded in their beginning like a flame bound to a coal."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {7 "גולת הכותרת translated literally as 'the crown's crown' (lit. 'the bowl/globe of the capital'); the phrase is unusual and rendered hyper-literally. Also, 'Eitan ha-Ezrahi in his book' (איתן האזרחי בספרו) is retained as written; it is uncertain whether this refers to a specific known work or to a poetic attribution."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:33.054720714Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}